BT /F1 12 Tf 72 712 Td (Cellular Biology: Structure and Function of Mitochondria. Mitochondria are double-membraned organelles found in most eukaryotic organisms. They generate most of the cell's supply of adenosine triphosphate (ATP), used as a source of chemical energy. The outer membrane covers the organelle and contains it like a skin. The inner membrane folds inward into structures called cristae, increasing the surface area for chemical reactions. Anaerobic respiration takes place in the cytoplasm, whereas aerobic respiration occurs in the mitochondria. Cells regulate metabolic activity through active and passive transport of nutrients.) Tj ET
